Yes — and the useful part is that the thing you've been doing by instinct has a literature, including one law that argues you were right to delete.

  The philosophy, named

  Lean software development — Mary & Tom Poppendieck, 2003. Where "lean" in software comes from: the Toyota Production System, ported. Two of its seven principles are doing real work in this
  repo already. Eliminate waste — anything built that hasn't reached a user is inventory, and inventory rots (the theme was inventory for four slices). Decide as late as possible — the
  "last responsible moment." Deferring the render path isn't indecision; it's the principle.

  Gall's Law — John Gall, Systemantics, 1975. The strongest defence of what you just did:

  ▎ A complex system that works is invariably found to have evolved from a simple system that worked. A complex system designed from scratch never works and cannot be patched up to make it 
  ▎ work.

  "Duplication is far cheaper than the wrong abstraction" — Sandi Metz, RailsConf 2014, expanded in her 2016 post The Wrong Abstraction. Your over-engineering rule is this sentence. Her
  insight is that the wrong abstraction is worse than none, because each new caller bends it further rather than replacing it.

  YAGNI and "do the simplest thing that could possibly work" — Extreme Programming, Beck/Jeffries, late 90s. The ancestors of the rule you wrote.

  Simple Made Easy — Rich Hickey, Strange Loop 2011. The one I'd actually press on you before you pick a render path. Simple means one braid — not interleaved. Easy means near at hand,
  familiar. They're different axes, and conflating them is how you end up with a framework. Liquid was easy (Shopify-shaped, theme authors know it) and not simple (it owned templating and
  escaping and i18n and asset URLs and file resolution — five concerns braided into one dependency). That's a sharper diagnosis of why it had to go than "too heavy."

  Chesterton's Fence — the counterweight, and the one that constrains me more than you. Don't remove what you don't understand the purpose of. It's why I flagged the timeout/network split as
  a cost rather than just deleting it quietly.

  A Philosophy of Software Design — John Ousterhout, 2018. Worth knowing it pushes back on you in two places: he wants deep modules (narrow interface, substantial behaviour) and warns that
  lean codebases drift into many shallow ones — more interface than substance. And he argues explicitly that "self-documenting code" is usually a rationalisation for under-commenting. Your
  comment rule survives that critique because it targets restatement, not explanation — but the strongest counterargument is real and respectable.

  One place your rules are more eager than the canon: Fowler's Rule of Three says wait for the third occurrence before abstracting. You wrote "second caller." That's a deliberate half-step
  tighter; fine, just know it's a choice.

  On defining our patterns

  The backend already sets the house shape, and its entry bar is the good part:

  ▎ a pattern earns a spot only when it's repeated ≥2× and stable

  Applying that bar honestly, the storefront has four today — and the reason it's safe to write them now, before deciding the render path, is that all four are render-agnostic and
  runtime-agnostic. They survive whatever you pick:

  1. The page-route shape — fetch → failure → gate → locale → answer. Repeated 4×. This is the one that most needs writing down, because a new route that reorders it doesn't look broken, it
  leaks.
  2. Decide locally before spending an origin request — shape and host checks run before any internal call, so a scan of the URL space can't be turned into origin amplification.
  3. Uniform refusal — a wrong host, an unpublished locale, and a locked shop all answer the same way regardless of what exists behind them. Three sites, one rule: a refusal must never be a
  disclosure.
  4. Opaque pass-through — the cursor, and now the whole payload. The backend owns the meaning; re-deriving it here is how the BFF stops being thin.

  What I'd deliberately leave out for failing the bar: the internal-client shape (one module), the tenant-scoped signed cookie (one use), 404-vs-502 (one place). They go in when a second
  instance shows up.

  I'd also keep the split the backend keeps: CLAUDE.md = rules (your four), PATTERNS.md = recipes, STACK.md = versions. Patterns are "here's the shape to copy," not "here's what you must
  do."

  Want me to write PATTERNS.md with those four? It'd be short — roughly a page — and I'd resist padding it to look substantial.
